{"causes":["The rise of Vedic society in ancient India","The development of Hindu philosophy during the Vedic period (1500 BCE - 500 BCE)","The composition of Sanskrit texts by various sages and seers"],"effects":["The shaping of Hindu thought and practice for centuries to come","The influence on Eastern philosophy and spirituality","The preservation of ancient Sanskrit poetry and drama"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"vedas","sourceName":"Vedas","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"upanishads","targetName":"Upanishads","context":"The Upanishads were composed by sages and seers who drew upon the teachings of the Vedas"},{"sourceSlug":"mahabharata","sourceName":"Mahabharata","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"upanishads","targetName":"Upanishads","context":"The Bhagavad Gita is part of the epic poem Mahabharata"},{"sourceSlug":"hinduism","sourceName":"Hinduism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"upanishads","targetName":"Upanishads","context":"The Upanishads have had a profound impact on the development of Hindu thought and practice"}],"places":[{"name":"Kashmir Valley","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Varanasi","role":"Major center of learning"},{"name":"Ganges River","role":"Site of important Upanishad recitations"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Upanishads","type":"Reference article","slug":"upanishads"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6113985","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upanishads"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Upanishads left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 107 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Upanishads (Sanskrit: उपनिषद्, Upaniṣad, from upa 'near' + ni 'down' + sad 'to sit' — 'sitting down near a teacher') are a collection of ancient Sanskrit texts that constitute the philosophical and metaphysical culmination of Vedic literature — the last layer (hence 'Vedānta', the 'end of the Vedas') of the Vedic corpus, composed c. 800–200 BCE (the principal Upanishads) and continuing into the medieval period (the later or minor Upanishads, of which over 100 exist). The principal (mukhya) Upanishads number approximately 13 (according to the most canonical listing: Bṛhadāraṇyaka, Chāndogya, Taittirīya, Aitareya, Kaṭha, Īśā, Kena, Muṇḍaka, Māṇḍūkya, Praśna, Śvetāśvatara, Kaushītaki, and Maitrī), composed in classical Sanskrit (some in mixed prose and verse, others in verse) and preserved within the four Vedas as their concluding philosophical commentaries.

The Upanishads are the foundational texts of Vedānta philosophy and of the Hindu understanding of the ultimate nature of reality. Their central philosophical teachings revolve around: Brahman (the ultimate, impersonal, infinite reality underlying all existence — 'that from which all beings are born, in which they live, and into which they return'); Ātman (the individual Self or soul — identical with Brahman in the non-dualist Advaita interpretation: 'tat tvam asi', 'that thou art'); Karma and rebirth; and Moksha (liberation from the cycle of death and rebirth through knowledge of the identity of Ātman and Brahman). The Upanishads contain the foundational philosophical statements of Hindu metaphysics — 'Aham Brahmāsmi' ('I am Brahman'), 'Tat tvam asi' ('That thou art'), 'Prajñānam Brahma' ('Consciousness is Brahman'), 'Ayam ātmā Brahma' ('This Self is Brahman') — the 'four great sayings' (mahāvākyas) that became the philosophical touchstones of Vedānta.

The Upanishads were translated into Persian by Prince Dara Shukoh (1657), into Latin by Anquetil-Duperron (from Dara Shukoh's Persian version, 1801–1802), and their reception by Schopenhauer — who called the Oupnek'hat (Anquetil-Duperron's translation) 'the solace of my life' — was instrumental in introducing Hindu philosophy to Western thought.","subjects":["Sanskrit Literature","Ancient Era","Vedānta","Hindu Philosophy","Indian Religion","Metaphysics","Oral Tradition","Religious Texts"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Upanishads have had a profound impact on the development of Hindu thought and practice, shaping India's cultural heritage for centuries to come.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
